/**
 * @page py_circuits QuantumCircuit Builder
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section py_circuit_intro Overview
 *
 * For an ergonomic, Pythonic workflow similar to Qiskit, use the `QuantumCircuit`
 * class to construct circuits programmatically in memory without writing QASM strings.
 *
 * @section py_circuit_basic Building and Running a Circuit
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * from maestro.circuits import QuantumCircuit
 *
 * qc = QuantumCircuit()
 *
 * # Build a Bell state
 * qc.h(0)
 * qc.cx(0, 1)
 * qc.measure([(0, 0), (1, 1)])  # (qubit, classical_bit) pairs
 *
 * # Execute with defaults
 * result = qc.execute(shots=1000)
 * print(result["counts"])  # {"00": ~500, "11": ~500}
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_circuit_gates Available Gates
 *
 * | Gate | Method | Parameters |
 * |------|--------|------------|
 * | Pauli-X | `qc.x(qubit)` | — |
 * | Pauli-Y | `qc.y(qubit)` | — |
 * | Pauli-Z | `qc.z(qubit)` | — |
 * | Hadamard | `qc.h(qubit)` | — |
 * | S | `qc.s(qubit)` | — |
 * | S† | `qc.sdg(qubit)` | — |
 * | T | `qc.t(qubit)` | — |
 * | T† | `qc.tdg(qubit)` | — |
 * | √X | `qc.sx(qubit)` | — |
 * | √X† | `qc.sxdg(qubit)` | — |
 * | K | `qc.k(qubit)` | — |
 * | Phase | `qc.p(qubit, λ)` | λ (radians) |
 * | Rx | `qc.rx(qubit, θ)` | θ (radians) |
 * | Ry | `qc.ry(qubit, θ)` | θ (radians) |
 * | Rz | `qc.rz(qubit, θ)` | θ (radians) |
 * | U | `qc.u(qubit, θ, φ, λ)` | 3 angles (Euler) |
 * | CNOT | `qc.cx(ctrl, tgt)` | — |
 * | CY | `qc.cy(ctrl, tgt)` | — |
 * | CZ | `qc.cz(ctrl, tgt)` | — |
 * | CH | `qc.ch(ctrl, tgt)` | — |
 * | CSX | `qc.csx(ctrl, tgt)` | — |
 * | CSX† | `qc.csxdg(ctrl, tgt)` | — |
 * | SWAP | `qc.swap(q1, q2)` | — |
 * | CP | `qc.cp(ctrl, tgt, λ)` | λ |
 * | CRx | `qc.crx(ctrl, tgt, θ)` | θ |
 * | CRy | `qc.cry(ctrl, tgt, θ)` | θ |
 * | CRz | `qc.crz(ctrl, tgt, θ)` | θ |
 * | CU | `qc.cu(ctrl, tgt, θ, φ, λ, γ)` | 4 angles |
 * | Toffoli | `qc.ccx(c1, c2, tgt)` | — |
 * | Fredkin | `qc.cswap(ctrl, q1, q2)` | — |
 * | Delay | `qc.delay(qubit, duration)` | duration in seconds |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_circuit_delay Delay and Idle Operations
 *
 * Physical idling periods can be scheduled on specific qubits using `qc.delay()`.
 * Durations are specified in seconds (e.g., `100e-9` for 100 ns, `50e-6` for 50 us).
 *
 * In noiseless simulation, delay acts as an identity. Under noisy simulation, it triggers
 * idle thermal relaxation (T1/T2), continuous-time Ornstein-Uhlenbeck phase drift, and
 * coherent detuning rotation according to the configured `NoiseModel` (see @ref py_noise).
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * # Append 50 microseconds of idle time on qubit 0
 * qc.delay(0, 50e-6)
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_circuit_measure Measurements
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * # Measure specific qubits to specific classical bits
 * qc.measure([(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2)])
 *
 * # Or measure all active qubits at once
 * qc.measure_all()
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section py_circuit_estimate Expectation Values with QuantumCircuit
 *
 * Expectation values can be estimated directly on a `QuantumCircuit` instance without adding measurement operations:
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * qc = QuantumCircuit()
 * qc.h(0)
 * qc.cx(0, 1)
 *
 * # Direct expectation values
 * result = qc.estimate(observables=["ZZ", "XX", "YY"])
 *
 * vals = result["expectation_values"]
 * print(f"<ZZ> = {vals[0]:.4f}")  #  1.0
 * print(f"<XX> = {vals[1]:.4f}")  #  1.0
 * print(f"<YY> = {vals[2]:.4f}")  # -1.0
 *
 * # With a specific backend config
 * mps_config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.MatrixProductState,
 *     max_bond_dimension=16,
 * )
 * result = qc.estimate(observables=["ZZ", "XX"], config=mps_config)
 * @endcode
 * @subsection py_circuits_transforms Pre-Execution Transforms and Reductions
 *
 * Maestro includes an extensible native transform pipeline that optimizes
 * circuits and observables before simulator allocation:
 *
 * 1. **Causal-cone reduction** (`enable_causal_cone_reduction=True`, default):
 *    Removes gates and idle qubits outside the combined backward dependencies of the
 *    requested observables.
 * 2. **Observable clustering** (`enable_observable_clustering=True`, default):
 *    Splits the observables into groups whose lightcones are disjoint and reduces
 *    and simulates each group on its own compact register. Observables whose
 *    lightcones overlap, even indirectly through other observables, stay in one
 *    group. Setting `max_cluster_qubits` also splits overlapping lightcones (see below).
 *    GPU Pauli propagation keeps all observables in one run.
 * 3. **Automatic parity-check reduction** (`auto_reduce=True`, default):
 *    Detects invariant check subspaces (e.g. QAOA parity ladders with uncomputed
 *    CNOT frames) and maps the simulation to an effective reduced register of
 *    dimension 2^rank(checks), simulated using the caller's configured backend.
 *
 * Any of these transforms can be disabled via `SimulatorConfig`:
 * @code{.py}
 * # Disable one or both transforms:
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     enable_causal_cone_reduction=False,
 *     auto_reduce=False,
 * )
 * result = qc.estimate(["ZZII", "IIZZ"], config=config)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Estimation results report diagnostic metadata on whether and how reduction was applied:
 * @code{.py}
 * result = qc.estimate(["ZZII", "IIZZ"])
 * print("Reduced:", result["auto_reduced"])
 * print("Qubits before:", result["qubits_before"])
 * print("Qubits after:", result["qubits_after"])
 * print("Reduction time (s):", result["reduction_time"])
 * print("Groups:", result["clusters_count"])
 * print("Largest group (qubits):", result["largest_cluster_qubits"])
 * print("Methods per group:", result["cluster_methods"])
 * @endcode
 *
 * Reduction runs before simulator allocation and uses the configured backend
 * and its options. Each group of observables gets its own reduced circuit;
 * `qubits_after` reports the largest. Expectation values come back in the
 * order the observables were given. The source circuit is unchanged.
 * GPU TensorNetwork estimates with a one-qubit effective register use GPU
 * Statevector on the selected device, because cuTensorNet's MPS factorization
 * requires at least two qubits. This also applies to single-observable requests
 * and one-qubit input circuits. Larger groups keep TensorNetwork; MPS and MPO
 * support one qubit directly. `cluster_methods` lists the actual SimulationType
 * integer values in group order, while `method` reports the first group's method.
 *
 * @subsubsection py_circuits_cluster_budget Bounding group size
 *
 * When many observables are spread across a chip, their lightcones often
 * chain together into one group covering every qubit, and clustering then
 * saves nothing. `max_cluster_qubits` caps each group's combined lightcone:
 * each group starts from the largest remaining lightcone and adds the
 * overlapping observable that adds the fewest new qubits, while the union
 * fits the budget. An observable whose own lightcone exceeds the budget gets
 * a group of its own. Disjoint lightcones are never grouped together.
 *
 * @code{.py}
 * config = maestro.SimulatorConfig(
 *     simulation_type=maestro.SimulationType.PauliPropagator,
 *     max_cluster_qubits=35,
 * )
 * result = qc.estimate(observables, config=config)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Smaller groups mean smaller registers but more simulator runs, so the best
 * budget depends on the backend. On a 118-qubit benchmark with 179 Pauli
 * observables, CPU Pauli propagation ran about 2.2x faster with budgets from
 * 35 to 50. A budget helps backends whose cost grows with register width
 * (Pauli propagation, statevector); matrix product states pay per group and
 * gain little. GPU Pauli propagation skips clustering and ignores the
 * budget: each GPU run costs about a second to set up, more than splitting saves.
 *
 * @note With PauliPropagator and `pp_gates_between_trims` above 1, trims land
 * on different gates in each group's reduced circuit, so values can change
 * slightly with the budget, within the truncation error. Trimming and
 * deduplicating after every gate (`pp_gates_between_trims=1`,
 * `pp_gates_between_deduplications=1`) makes values independent of the grouping.
 * Non-unitary operations (measurement, reset, quantum channels), classical control,
 * and distributed GPU layouts conservatively fall back to full execution.
 */
